 We complete the square for $x^2-6x+13$. Because the coefficient of $x^2$ is 1 and the constant term is 13, we can take half of $-6$ to get $-3$. We square $-3$ to get $9$ and call the original constant term 13. Therefore, we have \begin{align*}
x^2 -6x + 13 &= x^2 -6x + 9 + 13 - 9 \\
&= (x-3)^2 + 4.\end{align*} Because $(x-3)^2 \ge 0$ for all $x$, we know this expression is always nonnegative. The value of the expression is 4 when $x = \boxed{3}$. </think> <answer> 4 </answer>

