We label the segments of the triangle as in the diagram above. Assume that $a, b < 1$, as can occur only if both random numbers are less than 1.
Suppose first that $a < b$. Then $b < a + 1 < 2$, so $b$ is less than, say, $\frac{3}{4}$ of the time. And if $a \ge b$, then $b < a + 1 < 2$, so again $b$ is less than $\frac{3}{4}$ of the time. As for whether $a + b \ge a + 1 = 1 + b$, we want $b \ge \frac{1}{2}$ of the time. All together, for a triangle to be obtuse, the intervals when $b$ is $\le \frac{1}{2}$ of the time and $a$ is $> \frac{1}{2}$ of the time overlap. And the intervals when $b \ge \frac{3}{4}$ of the time and $a$ is $\le \frac{3}{4}$ of the time also overlap.
